\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{pair} & \\text{slope}\\\\ \\hline\n(1,1)-(2,2) & 1\\\\\n(1,2)-(3,1) & -\\tfrac12\\\\\n(1,3)-(2,1) & -2\\\\\n\\text{all other pairs} & 0,\\ \\infty,\\ \\text{or }-1\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThus the only slopes that are **allowed** for a sunny line are \\(1,\\,-\\tfrac12,\\,-2\\).  \nEach of these three slopes occurs for exactly one unordered pair of points,\nhence any sunny line can contain **at most two** points of \\(T_{3}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Consequences for a configuration with two sunny lines\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that three distinct lines \\(L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\) cover\nall points of \\(T_{3}\\) and that exactly two of them are sunny.\nRename so that  \n\n\\[\nS_{1},S_{2}\\text{ are sunny},\\qquad N\\text{ is the unique non\u2011sunny line}.\n\\]\n\nBecause each sunny line contains at most two points, the two sunny lines together\ncover at most four points of \\(T_{3}\\).  \nHence the non\u2011sunny line \\(N\\) must cover **at least two** points of \\(T_{3}\\).\n\nA non\u2011sunny line can be of one of the three types:\n\n* vertical: \\(x=c\\);\n* horizontal: \\(y=c\\);\n* slope \\(-1\\): \\(x+y=c\\).\n\nAll admissible constants are listed below together with the points of \\(T_{3}\\) they contain.\n\n| Type of \\(N\\) | Equation | Points of \\(T_{3}\\) on \\(N\\) |\n|----------------|----------|------------------------------|\n| vertical       | \\(x=1\\)  | \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\) |\n| vertical       | \\(x=2\\)  | \\((2,1),(2,2)\\) |\n| vertical       | \\(x=3\\)  | \\((3,1)\\) |\n| horizontal     | \\(y=1\\)  | \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\) |\n| horizontal     | \\(y=2\\)  | \\((1,2),(2,2)\\) |\n| horizontal     | \\(y=3\\)  | \\((1,3)\\) |\n| slope \\(-1\\)   | \\(x+y=2\\) | \\((1,1)\\) |\n| slope \\(-1\\)   | \\(x+y=3\\) | \\((1,2),(2,1)\\) |\n| slope \\(-1\\)   | \\(x+y=4\\) | \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\) |\n\nFor each possible \\(N\\) we examine the set \\(R=T_{3}\\setminus N\\) of points that\nmust be covered by the two sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Exhaustive analysis of the nine possibilities\n\nBelow we list, for each \\(N\\), the remaining points \\(R\\) and show that\n\\(R\\) cannot be covered by two sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n**(a) \\(N:x=1\\).**  \n\\(R=\\{(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\).  \nThe points \\((2,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\) share the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, so they\ncannot lie on a common sunny line (a sunny line cannot be vertical).  \nThus they must belong to different sunny lines.  \nThe third point \\((3,1)\\) cannot join the line through \\((2,1)\\) (that line would be horizontal) nor the line through \\((2,2)\\) (that line would have slope \\(-1\\)).  \nHence a third non\u2011sunny line would be required \u2013 contradiction.\n\n---\n\n**(b) \\(N:x=2\\).**  \n\\(R=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(3,1)\\}\\).  \nOnly the pair \\((1,2),(3,1)\\) has an admissible sunny slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\); all other\npairs have forbidden slopes.  \nConsequently one sunny line can cover \\((1,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\).  \nThe remaining two points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,3)\\) are vertical, so they cannot be\ntogether on a sunny line and would each need a separate line \u2013 impossible with only\none sunny line left.\n\n---\n\n**(c) \\(N:x=3\\).**  \n\\(R\\) contains five points, but two sunny lines can cover at most four; contradiction.\n\n---\n\n**(d) \\(N:y=1\\).**  \n\\(R=\\{(1,2),(1,3),(2,2)\\}\\).  \nNo two points of \\(R\\) have an admissible sunny slope (the three possible slopes are\nvertical, horizontal or \\(-1\\)).  Hence at most one point can be placed on a sunny\nline, leaving at least two uncovered \u2013 impossible.\n\n---\n\n**(e) \\(N:y=2\\).**  \n\\(R=\\{(1,1),(1,3),(2,1),(3,1)\\}\\).  \nThe only admissible sunny pair is \\((1,3),(2,1)\\) (slope \\(-2\\)).  \nThe remaining points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) are horizontal, so they cannot share a sunny line; they would each need a separate line \u2013 contradiction.\n\n---\n\n**(f) \\(N:y=3\\).**  \n\\(R\\) has five points \u2013 again exceeds the capacity of two sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n**(g) \\(N:x+y=2\\).**  \n\\(R\\) has five points \u2013 exceeds capacity.\n\n---\n\n**(h) \\(N:x+y=3\\).**  \n\\(R=\\{(1,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\).  \nOnly the pair \\((1,1),(2,2)\\) has an admissible sunny slope \\(1\\).  \nThe remaining points \\((1,3)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) have slope \\(-1\\) and cannot be placed\ntogether on a sunny line; they would each need a separate line \u2013 impossible.\n\n---\n\n**(i) \\(N:x+y=4\\).**  \n\\(R=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nNo two points of \\(R\\) have an admissible sunny slope (the three possible slopes\nare vertical, horizontal or \\(-1\\)).  Hence at most one point could be placed on a sunny line,\nleaving at least two uncovered \u2013 contradiction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nSince every admissible choice for the non\u2011sunny line leads to a contradiction,\nthere exists **no** set of three distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_{3}\\)\nwith exactly two sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3,\\;k=2\\text{ is impossible.}}\n\\]"
