            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nLet \\(n=3\\).  \nThe condition “for all positive integers \\(a,b\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)” yields the set  \n\n\\[\nT_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is not \\(0\\) (horizontal), not undefined (vertical), and not \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n---\n\n#### 1.  Admissible pairs of points  \n\nFor each unordered pair of points in \\(T_3\\) compute the slope.\n\n| Pair | Slope | Sunny? |\n|------|-------|--------|\n| \\((1,1),(2,2)\\) | \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{2-1}{2-1}=1\\) | yes |\n| \\((1,2),(3,1)\\) | \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{1-2}{3-1}=-\\frac12\\) | yes |\n| \\((1,3),(2,1)\\) | \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{1-3}{2-1}=-2\\) | yes |\n| all other pairs | \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) | no |\n\nThus the only pairs whose connecting line is sunny are  \n\n\\[\n\\{(1,1),(2,2)\\},\\qquad \\{(1,2),(3,1)\\},\\qquad \\{(1,3),(2,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nEach pair determines a distinct line, and each such line contains exactly two points of \\(T_3\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  The three sunny lines  \n\nFrom the admissible pairs we obtain the equations:\n\n1. Through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\):\n   \\[\n   L_1:\\; y = x .\n   \\]\n\n2. Through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\):\n   \\[\n   L_2:\\; y-2 = -\\frac12 (x-1)\\;\\Longrightarrow\\; y = -\\frac12 x + \\frac52 .\n   \\]\n\n3. Through \\((1,3)\\) and \\((2,1)\\):\n   \\[\n   L_3:\\; y-3 = -2 (x-1)\\;\\Longrightarrow\\; y = -2x + 5 .\n   \\]\n\nThe slopes are \\(1,\\,-\\tfrac12,\\,-2\\); none equals \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\), or \\(-1\\). Hence each line is sunny. The three slopes are distinct, so the lines are distinct.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Verification that every required point lies on a sunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c}\n\\text{Point} & \\text{Line containing it} & \\text{Check} \\\\ \\hline\n(1,1) & L_1 & 1 = 1 \\quad\\checkmark \\\\\n(2,2) & L_1 & 2 = 2 \\quad\\checkmark \\\\\n(1,2) & L_2 & 2 = -\\tfrac12\\cdot1 + \\tfrac52 = 2 \\quad\\checkmark \\\\\n(3,1) & L_2 & 1 = -\\tfrac12\\cdot3 + \\tfrac52 = 1 \\quad\\checkmark \\\\\n(1,3) & L_3 & 3 = -2\\cdot1 + 5 = 3 \\quad\\checkmark \\\\\n(2,1) & L_3 & 1 = -2\\cdot2 + 5 = 1 \\quad\\checkmark\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThus every point of \\(T_3\\) belongs to at least one of the three sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nWe have exhibited three distinct sunny lines that together contain all points required for \\(n=3\\). Therefore a configuration with exactly \\(k=3\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3,\\;k=3\\text{ is attainable.}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
