   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_0 &: y = x ,\\\\[2mm]\n   L_i &: y = x + i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,m-1),\\\\[2mm]\n   L_{-i}&: y = x - i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,m-1).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   For a fixed integer \\(c\\) with \\(|c|\\le m-1\\) the line \\(y=x+c\\) meets the triangle\n   \\(T_{2m-1}\\) in exactly \\(m-|c|\\) points, namely  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,1+c),\\;(2,2+c),\\dots ,\\;(m-|c|,\\,m-|c|+c).\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   The collection of all points appearing in (3) for the \\(2m-1\\) values of \\(c\\) is precisely the whole set \\(T_{2m-1}\\); each point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le 2m\\) has a unique difference \\(b-a\\) belonging to \\(\\{-m+1,\\dots ,m-1\\}\\), and it lies on the line \\(y=x+(b-a)\\).  \n   Moreover, every line in (2) has slope \\(1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore all of them are **sunny**.  \n   The number of points on each line is \\(m-|c|\\); summing over all \\(c\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{c=-(m-1)}^{m-1}(m-|c|)=m+(m-1)+(m-2)+\\dots +1+(m-1)+\\dots +1\n   =\\frac{(2m-1)(2m)}{2}=|T_{2m-1},\n   \\]\n\n   confirming that the lines are disjoint and cover every required point.\n\n   Hence for every odd \\(k=2m-1\\) we have an explicit family of \\(k\\) sunny lines covering all points of \\(T_k\\).\n\n5. **Putting the two parts together.**  \n   For a given \\(n\\ge 3\\) and a non\u2011negative integer \\(k\\le n\\) we proceed as follows.\n\n   *Use \\(n-k\\) diagonal lines* \\(x+y= s\\) with \\(s=k+2,k+3,\\dots ,n+1\\).  \n   These lines are non\u2011sunny and together cover all points of the original set whose sum exceeds \\(k+1\\).\n\n   *The remaining points* are exactly the set \\(T_k\\).  \n   By the previous paragraph they can be covered by \\(k\\) sunny lines **iff** \\(k\\) is odd (or \\(k=0\\)).  \n\n   Consequently the required configuration exists precisely for  \n\n   \\[\n   k=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad k\\text{ odd with }1\\le k\\le n .\n   \\]\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\