            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Reduction to the “small triangle’’  \n\nAssume we have a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the\nrequirements and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\nAmong the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines we may replace each of them by a\ndiagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) (parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\)); such a line is\nstill non‑sunny and still passes through all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) that\nlie on the original line, because any non‑sunny line is either vertical,\nhorizontal or diagonal, and each of those families can be represented by a\nsingle diagonal line after a suitable translation.\n\nChoose the \\(n-k\\) diagonal lines with equations  \n\n\\[\nx+y = s\\qquad (s=k+2,k+3,\\dots ,n+1).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(x+y=s\\) contains exactly \\(s-1\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\nHence the total number of points covered by the lines (2) equals  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{s=k+2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\sum_{t=k+1}^{n}t\n= \\frac{n(n+1)}2-\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the points that are **not** covered by (2) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal T_k:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\},\n\\qquad |\\mathcal T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus the original problem is equivalent to the following one:\n\n> For a given integer \\(k\\ge 0\\) does there exist a family of exactly \\(k\\)\n> sunny lines that covers all points of \\(\\mathcal T_k\\)?\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A bound for a sunny line inside \\(\\mathcal T_k\\)\n\nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line and let \\((a_1,b_1),\\dots ,(a_r,b_r)\\in\\mathcal T_k\\)\nlie on \\(L\\).\nIf two of these points had the same abscissa, \\(L\\) would be vertical;\nif two had the same ordinate, \\(L\\) would be horizontal;\nif two had the same sum \\(a+b\\), then the segment joining them would be\nparallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\), forcing \\(L\\) to be parallel to that line.\nBecause \\(L\\) is sunny, none of these coincidences can occur.\nHence the three sequences  \n\n\\[\na_1,\\dots ,a_r,\\qquad b_1,\\dots ,b_r,\\qquad a_1+b_1,\\dots ,a_r+b_r\n\\]\n\nare all pairwise distinct.\n\nIn \\(\\mathcal T_k\\) the possible values of the sum \\(a+b\\) are the\nintegers \\(2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\); there are exactly \\(k\\) of them.\nTherefore  \n\n\\[\nr\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe bound is sharp: the points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(2,2),\\dots ,\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor,\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr)\n\\]\n\nlie on the sunny line \\(y=x\\) and realise equality in (5).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Even \\(k\\) are impossible\n\nAssume \\(k\\) is even, say \\(k=2m\\;(m\\ge1)\\).\nFrom (5) each sunny line can contain at most \\(m\\) points of \\(\\mathcal T_k\\).\nWith only \\(k\\) sunny lines we could cover at most \\(km = 2m^2\\) points,\nwhereas  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 = \\frac{2m(2m+1)}2 = 2m^2+m > 2m^2 .\n\\]\n\nThus the points of \\(\\mathcal T_k\\) cannot be covered by \\(k\\) sunny lines.\nHence **no even \\(k>0\\) satisfies the requirements**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Construction for odd \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k=2m-1\\) with \\(m\\ge1\\).  \nConsider the family of \\(2m-1\\) lines\n\n\\[\nL_c:\\; y = x + c\\qquad\\text{for }c=-(m-1),-(m-2),\\dots ,0,\\dots ,m-1 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nEach line has slope \\(1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore all of them are sunny.\n\nFor a fixed \\(c\\) with \\(|c|\\le m-1\\) the integer points of \\(\\mathcal T_{2m-1}\\)\nlying on \\(L_c\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n(1,1+c),\\;(2,2+c),\\dots ,\\bigl(m-|c|,\\,m-|c|+c\\bigr) .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, the condition \\(a+b\\le 2m\\) together with \\(b=a+c\\) gives\n\\(2a+c\\le 2m\\), i.e. \\(a\\le m-|c|\\); the positivity of the ordinate\nrequires \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+c\\ge1\\), which is automatically satisfied for\nthe listed values of \\(a\\).\n\nConsequently line \\(L_c\\) contains exactly \\(m-|c|\\) points of \\(\\mathcal T_{2m-1}\\).\nSumming over all admissible \\(c\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{c=-(m-1)}^{m-1}(m-|c|)\n= m+(m-1)+\\dots +1+(m-1)+\\dots +1\n= \\frac{(2m-1)(2m)}2 = |\\mathcal T_{2m-1}|.\n\\]\n\nThus the points in (7) for the different values of \\(c\\) are disjoint and\ntheir union is the whole set \\(\\mathcal T_{2m-1}\\).  \nHence the \\(2m-1\\) sunny lines (6) cover every point of \\(\\mathcal T_{2m-1}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Completion of the original problem\n\nLet \\(n\\ge3\\) be given.\n\n* If \\(k=0\\) we simply take the \\(n\\) diagonal lines\n  \\(x+y=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\); all of them are non‑sunny and they cover\n  \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n* Let \\(k\\ge1\\).  \n  Use the \\(n-k\\) diagonal lines (2) to cover all points with\n  \\(a+b\\ge k+2\\).  \n  The remaining points are exactly \\(\\mathcal T_k\\).  \n  By Sections&nbsp;3 and&nbsp;4, \\(\\mathcal T_k\\) can be covered by \\(k\\) sunny\n  lines **iff** \\(k\\) is odd.  \n\nTherefore a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists **iff**\n\n\\[\nk=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad k\\text{ is odd and }1\\le k\\le n .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final Answer ###\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\text{ or }k\\text{ is odd with }1\\le k\\le n\\;}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
