ag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Reduction to the small triangle  \n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  \nAmong the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines we may (and shall) choose the diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\nx+y=s\\qquad (s=k+2,k+3,\\dots ,n+1) .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nEach line (2) is non‑sunny (it is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)) and contains precisely \\(s-1\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \nConsequently the points covered by the family (2) are\n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{s=k+2}^{n+1}\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\mid a+b=s\\},\n\\]\n\nand their total number is  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{s=k+2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2-\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nHence the points **not** covered by (2) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal T_k:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\},\n\\qquad |\\mathcal T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus the original problem is equivalent to\n\n> (**P\\(_k\\)**)  *Does there exist a family of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines that covers all points of \\(\\mathcal T_k\\)?*\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A sharp bound for a sunny line inside \\(\\mathcal T_k\\)\n\nLet a sunny line \\(L\\) contain the points  \n\n\\[\n(a_1,b_1),\\dots ,(a_r,b_r)\\in\\mathcal T_k .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(L\\) is not horizontal, the ordinates \\(b_i\\) are pairwise distinct; because it is not vertical, the abscissae \\(a_i\\) are pairwise distinct; and because it is not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), the sums \\(s_i:=a_i+b_i\\) are pairwise distinct.  \n\nIn \\(\\mathcal T_k\\) the possible sums are the integers  \n\n\\[\n2,3,\\dots ,k+1,\n\\]\n\ni.e. exactly \\(k\\) different values.  \nConsequently the set \\(\\{s_1,\\dots ,s_r\\}\\) is a subset of a set of size \\(k\\) and the three sequences \\((a_i)\\), \\((b_i)\\), \\((s_i)\\) are pairwise disjoint.  \n\nIf \\(r>\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) then at least \\(\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{r}{2}\\bigr\\rceil\\) of the sums would lie in the same half‑interval  \n\\(\\{2,\\dots ,\\lceil\\frac{k+2}{2}\\rceil\\}\\) or \\(\\{\\lceil\\frac{k+2}{2}\\rceil,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\).  \nTwo points whose sums belong to the same half‑interval would have either the same abscissa or the same ordinate, contradicting the three‑pairwise‑distinct condition.  \nHence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,r\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\,}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe bound is attained, for instance, by the points \\((1,1),(2,2),\\dots ,\\bigl(\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\rfloor,\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\rfloor\\bigr)\\) on the sunny line \\(y=x\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Even \\(k\\) are impossible  \n\nLet \\(k=2m\\) be even.  \nFrom (6) each sunny line can contain at most \\(m\\) points of \\(\\mathcal T_k\\).  \nWith only \\(k\\) sunny lines we could therefore cover at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\cdot m = 2m^{2}\n\\]\n\npoints, whereas  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2=\\frac{2m(2m+1)}2=2m^{2}+m>2m^{2}.\n\\]\n\nThus (**P\\(_k\\)**) has no solution for even \\(k>0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Construction for odd \\(k\\)\n\nWrite \\(k=2m-1\\) with \\(m\\ge1\\).  \nWe shall exhibit \\(2m-1\\) sunny lines that partition \\(\\mathcal T_{2m-1}\\) into \\(2m-1\\) disjoint subsets, each of size \\(m\\).\n\n---\n\n##### 4.1  The family of lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_t      &: y = x + (t-1)               && (t=1,2,\\dots ,m),\\\\[2mm]\nL_{m+t}  &: y = -\\frac{1}{t}\\,x + \\frac{2m}{t}\n          && (t=1,2,\\dots ,m-1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nAll slopes are different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); therefore every line in (7) is sunny.\n\n---\n\n##### 4.2  Points on the first \\(m\\) lines  \n\nFix \\(c=t-1\\) with \\(0\\le c\\le m-1\\).  \nThe integer points of \\(\\mathcal T_{2m-1}\\) satisfying \\(y=x+c\\) are\n\n\\[\n(1,1+c),\\;(2,2+c),\\dots ,\\bigl(m-c,\\;m\\bigr) .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThere are exactly \\(m-c\\) of them; in particular line \\(L_t\\) contains \\(m-(t-1)\\) points.\n\n---\n\n##### 4.3  Points on the remaining \\(m-1\\) lines  \n\nFor a fixed \\(t\\in\\{1,\\dots ,m-1\\}\\) the line \\(L_{m+t}\\) is given by  \n\n\\[\ny = -\\frac{1}{t}x + \\frac{2m}{t}\n\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\nx + t y = 2m .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(x,y\\) are positive integers, (9) forces  \n\n\\[\nx = 2m - t y ,\\qquad y = 1,2,\\dots ,m .\n\\]\n\nThus the admissible solutions are  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(2m-t,\\,1\\bigr),\\;\n\\bigl(2m-2t,\\,2\\bigr),\\;\n\\dots ,\\;\n\\bigl(t,\\,m\\bigr) .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nAll of them satisfy \\(x+y\\le 2m\\); consequently each line \\(L_{m+t}\\) contains exactly \\(m\\) points of \\(\\mathcal T_{2m-1}\\).\n\n---\n\n##### 4.4  Disjointness and completeness  \n\nThe points listed in (8) have non‑negative difference \\(b-a\\); the points listed in (10) have negative difference.  \nHence a point cannot belong to two different lines of the family (7).  \n\nCounting the points contributed by the two sub‑families we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{c=0}^{m-1}(m-c) \\;+\\; \\sum_{t=1}^{m-1} m\n= \\frac{m(m+1)}2 + m(m-1)\n= m(2m-1)\n= |\\mathcal T_{2m-1}|.\n\\]\n\nTherefore the \\(2m-1\\) sunny lines (7) cover **every** point of \\(\\mathcal T_{2m-1}\\).\n\n---\n\nThus (**P\\(_k\\)**) is solvable for every odd \\(k\\) (the case \\(k=1\\) corresponds to the single line \\(y=x\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Completion of the original problem  \n\nLet \\(n\\ge3\\) be given.\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  Take the \\(n\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\); all are non‑sunny and they cover \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n* **\\(k\\ge1\\) odd.**  \n  Use the \\(n-k\\) diagonal lines (2) to cover all points with sum \\(\\ge k+2\\).  \n  The remaining set is \\(\\mathcal T_k\\); by the construction of §4 it is covered by exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n* **\\(k\\) even, \\(k>0\\).**  \n  By §3 no family of \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover \\(\\mathcal T_k\\); consequently no configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.\n\nHence a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists **iff**\n\n\\[\nk=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad k\\text{ is odd and }1\\le k\\le n .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final Answer  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ k\\ \\text{odd with}\\ 1\\le k\\le n\\;}\n\\]"
