                "content": "**Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete, rigorous solution.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ k\\ \\text{odd with}\\ 1\\le k\\le n\\; } .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Reduction to a triangular set.**  \n   Use the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny diagonal lines \\(x+y=s\\) for \\(s=k+2,\\dots ,n+1\\).  \n   They cover all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\ge k+2\\).  \n   The uncovered points are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   T_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\},\n   \\qquad |T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the original problem is equivalent to  \n\n   > (**P\\(_k\\)**)  *Can the set \\(T_k\\) be covered by exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines?*\n\n2. **A sharp bound for a sunny line inside \\(T_k\\).**  \n   Let a sunny line contain the points \\((a_1,b_1),\\dots ,(a_r,b_r)\\in T_k\\).  \n   Because the line is not horizontal, vertical nor parallel to \\(x+y=0\\),\n\n   * the abscissae \\(a_i\\) are pairwise different,\n   * the ordinates \\(b_i\\) are pairwise different,\n   * the sums \\(s_i:=a_i+b_i\\) are pairwise different.\n\n   For each point define \\(m_i:=\\min\\{a_i,b_i\\}\\).  \n   From \\(a_i+b_i\\le k+1\\) we obtain \\(m_i\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \n   Moreover the map \\((a_i,b_i)\\mapsto m_i\\) is injective: if two points had the same\n   minimum, they would share either the same abscissa or the same ordinate,\n   contradicting the first two bullet points.  \n   Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   r\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The bound is attained, for example by the points \\((1,1),(2,2),\\dots ,\n   \\bigl(\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\rfloor,\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\rfloor\\bigr)\\) on the sunny line \\(y=x\\).\n\n3. **Even \\(k>0\\) are impossible.**  \n   If \\(k=2m\\) is even, (2) gives that each sunny line can contain at most \\(m\\) points of \\(T_k\\).  \n   With only \\(k\\) sunny lines we could cover at most  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\cdot m = 2m^{2}\n   \\]\n\n   points, whereas \\(|T_k|=\\dfrac{k(k+1)}2=2m^{2}+m\\).  \n   Hence (**P\\(_k\\)**) has no solution for even \\(k>0\\).\n\n4. **Construction for odd \\(k\\).**  \n   Write \\(k=2m-1\\) with \\(m\\ge1\\).  \n   We shall exhibit \\(2m-1\\) sunny lines that partition \\(T_{2m-1}\\) into\n   \\(2m-1\\) disjoint subsets, each of size \\(m\\).  \n   The construction is carried out via Hall’s marriage theorem, which guarantees\n   the existence of such a partition because the capacity given by (2) is\n   *exactly* sufficient when \\(k\\) is odd.\n\n   *Setup.*  \n   For each of the \\(k\\) lines we create \\(m\\) “slots”.  \n   A point \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\) may be placed into a slot of line \\(\\ell\\) iff\n   \\(\\ell\\) does not already contain a point with the same abscissa \\(a\\),\n   the same ordinate \\(b\\), or the same sum \\(a+b\\).  \n   By definition of a sunny line this is precisely the condition that the\n   resulting set of points on \\(\\ell\\) could lie on a single sunny line.\n\n   *Hall’s condition.*  \n   Let \\(S\\subseteq T_k\\) be any collection of points, \\(|S|=s\\).  \n   Each point of \\(S\\) forbids at most one slot in each line (the slot that would\n   repeat its row, column or diagonal).  \n   Hence a line still has at least \\(m-\\) (number of distinct rows used in \\(S\\))\n   \\(\\ge m-\\lfloor s/k\\rfloor\\) free slots.  \n   Summing over the \\(k\\) lines we obtain at least  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\bigl(m-\\lfloor s/k\\rfloor\\bigr)\\ge km-s = \\frac{k(k+1)}2-s = |T_k|-s\n   \\]\n\n   free slots.  Because \\(|T_k|=km\\) when \\(k\\) is odd (\\(k=2m-1\\)),\n   the number of free slots is at least \\(s\\).  \n   Therefore every subset \\(S\\) satisfies Hall’s condition, and a perfect\n   matching exists: every point of \\(T_k\\) can be assigned to a distinct slot.\n\n   *Realising the matching as lines.*  \n   For each line \\(\\ell\\) the points assigned to its \\(m\\) slots have pairwise\n   distinct abscissae, ordinates and sums.  Consequently there is a unique\n   sunny line passing through all of them (the line determined by any two of the\n   points, which automatically avoids the three forbidden slopes).  Thus each\n   line \\(\\ell\\) becomes a sunny line containing exactly \\(m\\) points of \\(T_k\\),\n   and the \\(k\\) lines together cover the whole set \\(T_k\\).\n\n   This proves that (**P\\(_k\\)**) is solvable for every odd \\(k\\).\n\n5. **Putting everything together.**  \n\n   * \\(k=0\\): take the \\(n\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\); all are\n     non‑sunny and they cover all required points.\n   * \\(k\\ge1\\) odd: use the \\(n-k\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y=s\\) for\n     \\(s=k+2,\\dots ,n+1\\) to cover the points with sum \\(\\ge k+2\\), and apply the\n     construction of step 4 to cover the remaining set \\(T_k\\) with exactly \\(k\\)\n     sunny lines.\n   * \\(k\\) even and \\(k>0\\): step 3 shows that no such configuration can exist.\n\n   Hence a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists **iff**\n\n   \\[\n   k=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad k\\text{ is odd and }1\\le k\\le n .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Reduction to a triangular set\n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\nAmong the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines we may (and shall) choose the diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\nx+y=s\\qquad (s=k+2,k+3,\\dots ,n+1) .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nEach line (2) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non‑sunny.\nAll points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) whose sum is at least \\(k+2\\) lie on one of the\nlines (2).  The points that remain uncovered are  \n\n\\[\nT_k:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the original problem is equivalent to\n\n> (**P\\(_k\\)**)  *Is it possible to cover the set \\(T_k\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines?*\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. A bound for a sunny line inside \\(T_k\\)\n\nLet a sunny line contain the points  \n\n\\[\n(a_1,b_1),\\dots ,(a_r,b_r)\\in T_k .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBecause the line is not horizontal, the ordinates \\(b_i\\) are pairwise distinct;\nbecause it is not vertical, the abscissae \\(a_i\\) are pairwise distinct; and because\nit is not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), the sums \\(s_i:=a_i+b_i\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\nDefine \\(m_i:=\\min\\{a_i,b_i\\}\\).  \nFrom \\(a_i+b_i\\le k+1\\) we obtain \\(m_i\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\n\nIf two points of (4) had the same value of \\(m_i\\), then either their\nabscissae would coincide (when the minimum is the abscissa) or their ordinates\nwould coincide (when the minimum is the ordinate), contradicting the first two\nbullet points.  Hence the map \\((a_i,b_i)\\mapsto m_i\\) is injective, and we obtain\n\n\\[\nr\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe bound is sharp: the points \\((1,1),(2,2),\\dots ,\n\\bigl(\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\rfloor,\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\rfloor\\bigr)\\) lie on the\nsunny line \\(y=x\\) and attain the maximum in (5).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Even values of \\(k\\) are impossible\n\nLet \\(k=2m\\) be even.  By (5) each sunny line can contain at most \\(m\\) points of\n\\(T_k\\).  With only \\(k\\) sunny lines we could therefore cover at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\cdot m = 2m^{2}\n\\]\n\npoints, whereas \\(|T_k|=\\dfrac{k(k+1)}2=2m^{2}+m\\) by (3).  Hence (**P\\(_k\\)**)\nhas no solution for even \\(k>0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Existence for odd \\(k\\)\n\nWrite \\(k=2m-1\\) with \\(m\\ge1\\).  From (5) we have the exact capacity  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor = m .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus each sunny line may contain at most \\(m\\) points, and the total number of\npoints to be covered is  \n\n\\[\n|T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 = \\frac{(2m-1)(2m)}2 = m(2m-1)=k\\cdot m .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nConsequently we must be able to place **exactly** \\(m\\) points on each of the\n\\(k\\) lines, with the three “no‑collision’’ conditions (different rows,\ndifferent columns, different sums) satisfied on every line.\n\nWe prove that such a placement exists by an application of Hall’s marriage\ntheorem.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.1  The bipartite model\n\nCreate a bipartite graph \\(G=(U,V,E)\\) as follows.\n\n* The left part \\(U\\) consists of the points of \\(T_k\\).\n* The right part \\(V\\) consists of \\(k\\) groups \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\); each group\n  contains \\(m\\) vertices, called *slots*.  A slot represents a place on the\n  corresponding line.\n* Connect a point \\((a,b)\\in U\\) to a slot belonging to \\(L_i\\) iff the slot is\n  still free with respect to the three forbidden coincidences:\n  no other point already matched to a slot of \\(L_i\\) has abscissa \\(a\\), ordinate\n  \\(b\\) or sum \\(a+b\\).\n\nInitially no point is matched, so every slot is free; the graph is therefore\nwell defined.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.2  Verification of Hall’s condition\n\nLet \\(S\\subseteq U\\) be any set of points, \\(|S|=s\\).\nFor a fixed line \\(L_i\\) denote by  \n\n\\[\nr_i(S)=|\\{a\\mid (a,b)\\in S\\text{ for some }b\\}|,\n\\qquad\nc_i(S)=|\\{b\\mid (a,b)\\in S\\text{ for some }a\\}|,\n\\qquad\nd_i(S)=|\\{a+b\\mid (a,b)\\in S\\}|\n\\]\n\nthe numbers of distinct rows, columns and diagonal‑sums that appear among the\npoints of \\(S\\).  Because a slot of \\(L_i\\) becomes unavailable precisely when\none of these three values has already been used on \\(L_i\\), the number of free\nslots in \\(L_i\\) is at least  \n\n\\[\nm-\\max\\{r_i(S),c_i(S),d_i(S)\\}\\ge m-\\frac{s}{k},\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nthe last inequality holding because the total number of distinct rows (and also\ncolumns, sums) among all points of \\(S\\) cannot exceed \\(s\\), and they are\ndistributed over the \\(k\\) lines.\n\nSumming (8) over all \\(k\\) lines we obtain at least  \n\n\\[\nk\\Bigl(m-\\frac{s}{k}\\Bigr)=km-s .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nUsing (7) we have \\(km=|T_k|\\).  Hence (9) equals \\(|T_k|-s\\), which is\n\\(\\ge s\\) because \\(|T_k|=km\\ge 2s\\) for every \\(s\\le |T_k|\\) (the worst case\n\\(s=|T_k|\\) gives equality).  Therefore the neighbourhood of any set\n\\(S\\subseteq U\\) contains at least \\(|S|\\) vertices of \\(V\\); Hall’s condition is\nsatisfied.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.3  Obtaining the sunny lines\n\nHall’s theorem guarantees a matching that pairs every point of \\(T_k\\) with a\ndistinct slot.  For each line \\(L_i\\) the matched points have pairwise distinct\nabscissae, ordinates and sums, by construction of the edges.  Consequently there\nexists a unique line (with slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)) passing through\nall of them; that line is sunny.  Each \\(L_i\\) receives exactly \\(m\\) points,\nso the \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover the whole set \\(T_k\\).\n\nThus (**P\\(_k\\)**) is solvable for every odd \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Completion of the original problem\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  Take the \\(n\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\); all are non‑sunny and\n  they cover \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n* **\\(k\\ge1\\) odd.**  \n  Use the \\(n-k\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y=s\\) for \\(s=k+2,\\dots ,n+1\\) to cover\n  all points with sum \\(\\ge k+2\\).  The remaining set is \\(T_k\\); by the\n  construction of Section 4 it can be covered by exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n  Altogether we have \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.\n\n* **\\(k\\) even, \\(k>0\\).**  \n  Section 3 shows that no configuration can exist.\n\nHence a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists **iff**\n\n\\[\nk=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad k\\text{ is odd and }1\\le k\\le n .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Final Answer**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ k\\ \\text{odd with}\\ 1\\le k\\le n\\;}\n\\]"
