).  With only \\(k\\) sunny lines we could cover at most  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\cdot m = 2m^{2}\n   \\]\n\n   points, whereas \\(|T_k|=\\dfrac{k(k+1)}2=2m^{2}+m\\) by (1).  Thus (**P\\(_k\\)**)\n   has no solution for even \\(k>0\\).\n\n4. **Construction for odd \\(k\\).**  \n   Write \\(k=2m-1\\) (\\(m\\ge1\\)).  By (2) each sunny line can contain at most\n   \\(m\\) points of \\(T_k\\), and (1) gives \\(|T_k|=km=m(2m-1)\\); consequently we\n   must place **exactly** \\(m\\) points on each of the \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   We realise this placement with Hall\u2019s marriage theorem.\n\n   *Bipartite model.*  \n   The left part \\(U\\) consists of the points of \\(T_k\\).  \n   The right part \\(V\\) consists of \\(k\\) groups \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\); each group\n   contains \\(m\\) vertices, called *slots*.  A point \\((a,b)\\in U\\) is joined\n   to a slot of \\(L_i\\) iff, among the points already assigned to \\(L_i\\), none\n   has abscissa \\(a\\), ordinate \\(b\\) or sum \\(a+b\\).  (Initially no point is\n   assigned, so every slot is adjacent to every point.)\n\n   *Verification of Hall\u2019s condition.*  \n   Let \\(S\\subseteq U\\) with \\(|S|=s\\).  For a fixed line \\(L_i\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   r_i(S)=|\\{a\\mid (a,b)\\in S\\}|,\\qquad\n   c_i(S)=|\\{b\\mid (a,b)\\in S\\}|,\\qquad\n   d_i(S)=|\\{a+b\\mid (a,b)\\in S\\}|.\n   \\]\n\n   At most one of the three numbers can be larger than the number of slots that\n   become unavailable in \\(L_i\\); therefore the number of free slots in \\(L_i\\)\n   is at least  \n\n   \\[\n   m-\\max\\{r_i(S),c_i(S),d_i(S)\\}\\ge m-\\frac{s}{k},\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   because the total of the three quantities over all \\(k\\) lines does not exceed\n   \\(s\\).  Summing (3) over the \\(k\\) lines yields at least  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\Bigl(m-\\frac{s}{k}\\Bigr)=km-s=|T_k|-s\n   \\]\n\n   free slots.  Since \\(|T_k|=km\\), the neighbourhood of \\(S\\) contains at\n   least \\(|S|\\) vertices of \\(V\\).  Hall\u2019s condition holds for every \\(S\\).\n\n   *Obtaining the lines.*  \n   Hall\u2019s theorem gives a perfect matching that assigns each point of \\(T_k\\) to a\n   distinct slot.  For a fixed \\(L_i\\) the assigned points have pairwise distinct\n   abscissae, ordinates and sums; consequently there exists a unique line (with\n   slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)) passing through all of them.  That line\n   is sunny and contains exactly \\(m\\) points of \\(T_k\\).  Doing this for every\n   \\(L_i\\) yields \\(k\\) sunny lines covering the whole set \\(T_k\\).\n\n5. **Completion of the original problem.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Take the \\(n\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\); all are\n   non\u2011sunny and they cover every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b>0,\n   a+b\\le n+1\\}\\).\n\n   *\\(k\\ge1\\) odd.*  Use the \\(n-k\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y=s\\) for\n   \\(s=k+2,\\dots ,n+1\\); they cover all points with sum \\(\\ge k+2\\).  The\n   remaining set is \\(T_k\\); by step\u202f4 it can be covered by exactly \\(k\\) sunny\n   lines.  Altogether we have \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny.\n\n   *\\(k\\) even, \\(k>0\\).*  Step\u202f3 shows that the lower\u2011triangle set \\(T_k\\) cannot\n   be covered by \\(k\\) sunny lines, hence no configuration with exactly \\(k\\)\n   sunny lines exists.\n\n   Therefore a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists **iff**\n\n   \\[\n   k=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad k\\ \\text{odd with}\\ 1\\le k\\le n .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Final Answer**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ k\\ \\text{odd with}\\ 1\\le k\\le n\\;}\n\\]"
