 this case the two sums are equal:\n      \\[\n      a+b' = m+b' = a'+b = a'+m .\n      \\]\n      This contradicts the third bullet (the sums must be distinct).  \n\n   Hence two different points cannot have the same minimum, proving the claim.  \n\n   Because the minima are distinct and each lies in \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,\n   \\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\rfloor\\}\\), we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   r\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The bound is attained, for example by the points\n   \\((1,1),(2,2),\\dots ,\\bigl(\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\rfloor,\n   \\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\rfloor\\bigr)\\) on the sunny line \\(y=x\\).\n\n3. **Even \\(k>0\\) are impossible.**  \n   Let \\(k=2m\\) be even.  By (2) each sunny line can contain at most \\(m\\)\n   points of \\(T_{k}\\).  With only \\(k\\) sunny lines we could cover at most  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\cdot m = 2m^{2}\n   \\]\n\n   points, whereas \\(|T_{k}|=\\dfrac{k(k+1)}2 = 2m^{2}+m\\) by (1).  Hence (**P\\(_k\\)**)\n   has no solution for even \\(k>0\\).\n\n4. **Construction for odd \\(k\\).**  \n   Write \\(k=2m-1\\) with \\(m\\ge1\\).  From (2) each sunny line can contain at most\n   \\(m\\) points of \\(T_{k}\\); because \\(|T_{k}|=km=m(2m-1)\\) (see (1)), a\n   covering with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines must use **exactly** \\(m\\) points on\n   each line.\n\n   We give an explicit family of \\(k\\) sunny lines, each containing precisely\n   \\(m\\) points of \\(T_{k}\\).\n\n   *Definition of the lines.*  \n   For \\(t=0,1,\\dots ,m-1\\) set  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{2t+1}:\\; y = x + t ,                                   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   and for \\(t=1,2,\\dots ,m-1\\) set  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{2t}:\\; y = -2x + (2m+1-t).                              \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   All slopes are \\(1\\) or \\(-2\\); neither is \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\), so\n   every line is sunny.  The lines are distinct because they have different\n   intercepts.\n\n   *Points on the lines.*  \n   \u2013 **Lines of type (3).**  \n     A point \\((x,y)\\) lies on \\(L_{2t+1}\\) iff \\(y=x+t\\).  \n     The conditions \\(x>0,\\;y>0\\) and \\(x+y\\le 2m\\) become  \n\n     \\[\n     x\\ge 1,\\qquad x+t\\ge 1,\\qquad 2x+t\\le 2m .\n     \\]\n\n     The last inequality gives \\(x\\le m-\\frac{t}{2}\\).  \n     Since \\(t\\) is an integer, the admissible integers are  \n\n     \\[\n     x=1,2,\\dots ,m-t .\n     \\]\n\n     Hence \\(L_{2t+1}\\) contains exactly \\(m-t\\) points of \\(T_{k}\\).\n\n   \u2013 **Lines of type (4).**  \n     A point \\((x,y)\\) lies on \\(L_{2t}\\) iff \\(y=-2x+ (2m+1-t)\\).  \n     The positivity and triangle conditions become  \n\n     \\[\n     x\\ge 1,\\qquad -2x+ (2m+1-t)\\ge 1,\\qquad\n     x+(-2x+2m+1-t)\\le 2m .\n     \\]\n\n     The second inequality gives \\(x\\le m-\\frac{t}{2}\\); the third is\n     automatically satisfied once the second holds.  \n     Consequently the admissible integers are again  \n\n     \\[\n     x=1,2,\\dots ,m-t .\n     \\]\n\n     For each such \\(x\\) we obtain a distinct point\n     \\((x,\\; -2x+2m+1-t)\\); all of them belong to \\(T_{k}\\).  \n     Thus \\(L_{2t}\\) also contains exactly \\(m-t\\) points of \\(T_{k}\\).\n\n   *Counting the points.*  \n   For a fixed \\(t\\) the line \\(L_{2t+1}\\) contributes \\(m-t\\) points and the\n   line \\(L_{2t}\\) (when it exists) contributes the same number \\(m-t\\).  \n   Summing over \\(t\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{t=0}^{m-1}(m-t)+\\sum_{t=1}^{m-1}(m-t)\n   =\\bigl(m+(m-1)+\\dots +1\\bigr)+\\bigl((m-1)+\\dots +1\\bigr)\n   =m^{2}+(m-1)^{2}=m(2m-1)=km .\n   \\]\n\n   This equals \\(|T_{k}|\\); therefore the \\(k\\) lines listed in (3)\u2013(4) cover\n   **all** points of \\(T_{k}\\), each line containing exactly \\(m\\) points.\n   Hence a configuration satisfying (**P\\(_k\\)**) exists for every odd\n   \\(k=2m-1\\).\n\n5. **Completion of the original problem.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Take the \\(n\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\); all are\n   non