e \\(x=y\\).  \n      The line through \\((m,b)\\) and \\((a,m)\\) then has slope \\(-1\\), contradicting the\n      hypothesis that the line is sunny.  \u220e\n\n   Because the minima are distinct and belong to the set\n   \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\rfloor\\}\\), we obtain\n\n   \\[\n   r\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The bound is attained, for example by the points\n   \\((1,1),(2,2),\\dots ,\\bigl(\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\rfloor,\n   \\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\rfloor\\bigr)\\) on the sunny line \\(y=x\\).\n\n3. **Even \\(k>0\\) are impossible.**  \n   If \\(k=2t\\) is even, (2) gives that each sunny line contains at most \\(t\\) points of \\(T_{k}\\).  \n   Consequently the \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(k\\cdot t=2t^{2}\\) points, whereas by (1)\n\n   \\[\n   |T_{k}|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2=2t^{2}+t>2t^{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   Hence (**P\\(_k\\)**) has no solution for even \\(k>0\\).\n\n4. **Construction for odd \\(k\\).**  \n   Write \\(k=2t-1\\) with \\(t\\ge1\\).  \n   By (2) each sunny line can contain at most \\(t\\) points of \\(T_{k}\\); because\n   \\(|T_{k}|=k(k+1)/2=(2t-1) t\\), a covering with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines must\n   have **exactly** \\(t\\) points on each line.\n\n   We exhibit \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines, each containing precisely \\(t\\) points of\n   \\(T_{k}\\).\n\n   *Definition of the lines.*  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_{i}&:\\;y = x + i \\qquad &&(i=0,1,\\dots ,t-1),\\\\[2pt]\n   M_{i}&:\\;y = -2x + (2t+i) \\qquad &&(i=1,2,\\dots ,t-1).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   The slopes are \\(1\\) and \\(-2\\); neither equals \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\), so\n   every line in (3) is sunny, and all lines are distinct because they have different\n   intercepts.\n\n   *Points on the lines.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_{k}\\) (so \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le2t\\)).\n\n   **Case\u202f1 \u2013 \\(b\\ge a\\).**  \n   Put \\(d=b-a\\) (\\(d\\ge0\\)).  \n   From \\(a+b\\le2t\\) we obtain \\(a\\le t-\\frac d2\\); in particular \\(d\\le t-1\\).  \n   Hence \\(d\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,t-1\\}\\) and the point satisfies the equation\n   \\(b=a+d\\), i.e. it lies on the line \\(L_{d}\\).  \n   Moreover the inequality \\(a\\le t-\\frac d2\\) is exactly the condition that\n   \\(a\\) be an admissible integer on \\(L_{d}\\); therefore every point with\n   \\(b\\ge a\\) belongs to one of the lines \\(L_{0},L_{1},\\dots ,L_{t-1}\\).\n\n   **Case\u202f2 \u2013 \\(b<a\\).**  \n   Put \\(i = b+2a-2t\\).  \n   Because \\(a+b\\le2t\\) we have \\(i\\ge1\\); because \\(b\\ge1\\) we have\n   \\(i\\le t-1\\).  A short computation shows that the equality\n   \\(b = -2a + (2t+i)\\) is equivalent to \\(i = b+2a-2t\\); consequently the point\n   satisfies the equation of the line \\(M_{i}\\).  \n   The inequalities \\(a\\ge i\\) (which follows from \\(b\\ge1\\)) and\n   \\(a\\le t-\\frac{i}{2}\\) (which follows from \\(a+b\\le2t\\)) are precisely the\n   integer\u2011range conditions for a point to lie on \\(M_{i}\\).  Hence every point with\n   \\(b<a\\) lies on exactly one of the lines \\(M_{1},\\dots ,M_{t-1}\\).\n\n   *Number of points per line.*  \n   For a fixed \\(i\\) the admissible integers \\(a\\) on \\(L_{i}\\) are\n   \\(1\\le a\\le\\bigl\\lfloor t-\\frac i2\\bigr\\rfloor\\); therefore\n   \\(|L_{i}\\cap T_{k}| = \\bigl\\lfloor t-\\frac i2\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\n   For a fixed \\(i\\) the admissible integers \\(a\\) on \\(M_{i}\\) are\n   \\(i\\le a\\le\\bigl\\lfloor t-\\frac i2\\bigr\\rfloor\\); consequently\n   \\(|M_{i}\\cap T_{k}| = \\bigl\\lfloor t-\\frac i2\\bigr\\rfloor - i +1\n   = \\bigl\\lfloor t-\\frac i2\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\n   In both families the number of points equals \\(t-\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac i2\\bigr\\rfloor\\);\n   summing over all lines we obtain\n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=0}^{t-1}\\!\\bigl(t-\\lfloor i/2\\rfloor\\bigr)\n   +\\sum_{i=1}^{t-1}\\!\\bigl(t-\\lfloor i/2\\rfloor\\bigr)\n   =t^{2}+(t-1)^{2}=t(2t-1)=|T_{k}|.\n   \\]\n\n   Hence each of the \\(k=2t-1\\) lines contains exactly \\(t\\) points and together\n   they cover **all** points of \\(T_{k}\\).\n\n5. **Completion of the original problem.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Take the \\(n\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\); all are\n   non\u2011sunny and they cover every required point.\n\n   *\\(k\\ge1\\) odd.*  Let \\(k\\) be odd and write \\(k=2t-1\\).  \n   Use the \\(n-k\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y=s\\) for \\(s=k+2,\\dots ,n+1\\); they cover\n   all points with sum \\(\\ge k+2\\).  \n   The remaining points are exactly \\(T_{k}\\); by the construction of step\u202f4 they\n   are covered by \\(k\\) sunny lines.  Altogether we have \\(n\\) distinct lines,\n   exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny.\n\n   *\\(k\\) even, \\(k>0\\).*  Step\u202f3 shows that \\(T_{k}\\) cannot be covered by\n   \\(k\\) sunny lines, therefore no configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines\n   exists.\n\n   Consequently a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists **iff**\n\n   \\[\n   k=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad k\\ \\text{odd with}\\ 1\\le k\\le n .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Final Answer**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ k\\ \\text{odd with}\\ 1\\le k\\le n\\;}\n\\]"
