            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have corrected the solution.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ k\\ \\text{odd with}\\ 1\\le k\\le n\\; } .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Reduction to a triangular set.**  \n   Use the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines \\(x+y=s\\;(s=k+2,\\dots ,n+1)\\).  \n   They cover every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\ge k+2\\).  \n   The remaining points are  \n\n   \\[\n   T_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\},\n   \\qquad |T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the problem is equivalent to  \n\n   > (**P\\(_k\\)**) *Can the set \\(T_k\\) be covered by exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny\n   lines?*\n\n2. **A sharp bound for a sunny line inside \\(T_k\\).**  \n   Let a sunny line contain points \\((a_1,b_1),\\dots ,(a_r,b_r)\\in T_k\\).  \n   Because the line is not horizontal, vertical nor of slope \\(-1\\),\n\n   * the abscissae \\(a_i\\) are pairwise different,\n   * the ordinates \\(b_i\\) are pairwise different,\n   * the sums \\(a_i+b_i\\) are pairwise different.\n\n   Define \\(m_i=\\min\\{a_i,b_i\\}\\).  \n   From \\(a_i+b_i\\le k+1\\) we have \\(m_i\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\n\n   **Lemma 1.** The map \\((a_i,b_i)\\mapsto m_i\\) is injective.  \n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Suppose two distinct points of the line have the same minimum \\(m\\).  \n   Three possibilities arise:\n\n   1. Both have abscissa \\(m\\) – impossible because the abscissae are distinct.  \n   2. Both have ordinate \\(m\\) – impossible because the ordinates are distinct.  \n   3. One point is \\((m,b)\\) with \\(b>m\\) and the other is \\((a,m)\\) with \\(a>m\\).  \n      Their sums are \\(m+b\\) and \\(a+m\\); equality of the sums would give \\(a=b\\),  \n      which would make the line pass through \\((m,b)\\) and \\((a,m)\\) have slope \\(-1\\),  \n      contradicting the hypothesis that the line is sunny. ∎\n\n   Because the minima are distinct and belong to \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\rfloor\\}\\),\n\n   \\[\n   r\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The bound is attained, for example by the points\n   \\((1,1),(2,2),\\dots ,\\bigl(\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\rfloor,\n   \\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\rfloor\\bigr)\\) on the sunny line \\(y=x\\).\n\n3. **Even \\(k>0\\) are impossible.**  \n   If \\(k=2t\\) is even, (2) gives that each sunny line contains at most \\(t\\) points of\n   \\(T_k\\).  Consequently the \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(k\\cdot t=2t^{2}\\) points,\n   whereas by (1)\n\n   \\[\n   |T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2=2t^{2}+t>2t^{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   Hence (**P\\(_k\\)**) has no solution for even \\(k>0\\).\n\n4. **Construction for odd \\(k\\).**  \n   Write \\(k=2t-1\\) with \\(t\\ge1\\).  \n   By (2) each sunny line can contain at most \\(t\\) points of \\(T_k\\); because\n   \\(|T_k|=t(2t-1)=k\\cdot t\\), a covering with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines must have\n   **exactly** \\(t\\) points on each line.  We now exhibit a partition of \\(T_k\\) into\n   \\(2t-1\\) sunny lines, each containing precisely \\(t\\) points.\n\n   --------------------------------------------------------------------\n   **Definition of the lines.**  \n\n   *For \\(i=0,1,\\dots ,t-1\\) set*  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;y = x + i . \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   *For \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,t-1\\) set*  \n\n   \\[\n   M_j:\\;y = -2x + (2t+j). \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   All lines in (3)–(4) are sunny: their slopes are \\(1\\) and \\(-2\\), neither \\(0\\),\n   \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\).  They are distinct because they have different intercepts.\n\n   --------------------------------------------------------------------\n   **Points belonging to the lines.**  \n\n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\); thus \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le2t\\).\n\n   *Case 1 – \\(b\\ge a\\).*  \n   Put \\(i=b-a\\) (so \\(0\\le i\\le t-1\\) because \\(b-a\\le b\\le2t-a\\) and \\(a\\ge1\\)).  \n   Then \\(b=a+i\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_i\\).  \n   Moreover \\(a\\le t-\\frac i2\\) follows from \\(a+b\\le2t\\); consequently\n   \\(a\\) is an admissible integer on \\(L_i\\).  Hence every point with \\(b\\ge a\\)\n   lies on exactly one of the lines \\(L_0,\\dots ,L_{t-1}\\).\n\n   *Case 2 – \\(b<a\\).*  \n   Put \\(j = b+2a-2t\\).  \n   Because \\(a+b\\le2t\\) we have \\(j\\ge1\\); because \\(b\\ge1\\) we have\n   \\(j\\le t-1\\).  Rearranging the equality \\(j = b+2a-2t\\) gives\n   \\(b = -2a + (2t+j)\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(M_j\\).  \n   The inequalities \\(a\\ge j\\) (which follows from \\(b\\ge1\\)) and\n   \\(a\\le t-\\frac j2\\) (which follows from \\(a+b\\le2t\\)) are precisely the\n   integer‑range conditions for a point to belong to \\(M_j\\).  Hence every point\n   with \\(b<a\\) lies on exactly one of the lines \\(M_1,\\dots ,M_{t-1}\\).\n\n   The two cases are exhaustive, so every point of \\(T_k\\) belongs to one of the\n   lines listed in (3)–(4), and no point belongs to two different lines\n   (otherwise we would have both \\(b\\ge a\\) and \\(b<a\\), a contradiction).\n\n   --------------------------------------------------------------------\n   **Number of points on each line.**  \n\n   *Line \\(L_i\\).*  \n   On \\(L_i\\) we have points \\((a,a+i)\\) with \\(a\\ge1\\) and\n   \\(2a+i\\le2t\\); thus \\(1\\le a\\le t-\\bigl\\lfloor i/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   |L_i\\cap T_k| = t-\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac i2\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   *Line \\(M_j\\).*  \n   On \\(M_j\\) we have points \\((a, -2a+2t+j)\\) with \\(a\\ge1\\) and\n   \\(-2a+2t+j\\ge1\\); the latter inequality gives \\(a\\le t-\\frac j2\\).  \n   Together with \\(a\\ge j\\) (coming from the positivity of the ordinate) we obtain\n   \\(j\\le a\\le t-\\bigl\\lfloor j/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\), whence  \n\n   \\[\n   |M_j\\cap T_k| = t-\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac j2\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Because the right‑hand sides of (5) and (6) are exactly \\(t\\) when\n   \\(i\\) (or \\(j\\)) is even and \\(t-1\\) when it is odd, we compute the total\n   number of points covered:\n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=0}^{t-1}\\!\\bigl(t-\\lfloor i/2\\rfloor\\bigr)\n   +\\sum_{j=1}^{t-1}\\!\\bigl(t-\\lfloor j/2\\rfloor\\bigr)\n   =t^{2}+(t-1)^{2}=t(2t-1)=|T_k|.\n   \\]\n\n   Since the sets are disjoint, each of the \\(2t-1\\) lines contains exactly\n   \\(t\\) points of \\(T_k\\).  Thus the family \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=0}^{t-1}\\cup\n   \\{M_j\\}_{j=1}^{t-1}\\) is a required covering.\n\n5. **Completion of the original problem.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Take the \\(n\\) non‑sunny diagonal lines\n   \\(x+y=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\); they cover every required point.\n\n   *\\(k\\ge1\\) odd.*  Write \\(k=2t-1\\).  \n   Use the \\(n-k\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y=s\\) for \\(s=k+2,\\dots ,n+1\\); they cover\n   all points with sum \\(\\ge k+2\\).  The remaining points are exactly \\(T_k\\);\n   by step 4 they are covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines constructed there.\n   Altogether we have \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny.\n\n   *\\(k\\) even, \\(k>0\\).*  Step 3 shows that \\(T_k\\) cannot be covered by\n   \\(k\\) sunny lines, hence no configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines\n   exists.\n\n   Therefore a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists **iff**\n\n   \\[\n   k=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad k\\ \\text{odd with}\\ 1\\le k\\le n .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Final Answer**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ k\\ \\text{odd with}\\ 1\\le k\\le n\\;}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
