7}\n   \\]\n\n   The two inequalities together imply \\(|t|\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \n   Combining this with (6) yields the universal bound  \n\n   \\[\n   r\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   The bound is sharp: the line \\(y=-2x+2t\\) (slope \\(-2\\)) attains\n   \\(\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\rfloor\\) points when \\(k=2t-1\\).\n\n3. **Impossibility for even \\(k>0\\).**  \n   For even \\(k=2t\\) we have \\(\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\rfloor=t\\).  \n   By (8) each sunny line can cover at most \\(t\\) points of \\(T_k\\); with\n   exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines we can cover at most \\(k\\cdot t=2t^{2}\\) points.\n   Yet \\(|T_k|=\\dfrac{k(k+1)}2=2t^{2}+t>2t^{2}\\) by (1).  \n   Hence (**P\\(_k\\)**) is impossible for even \\(k>0\\).\n\n4. **Construction for odd \\(k\\).**  \n   Let \\(k=2t-1\\) (\\(t\\ge1\\)).  \n   We exhibit \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that partition \\(T_k\\).\n\n   *Lines of slope \\(+1\\).*  \n   For \\(i=0,1,\\dots ,t-1\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;y=x+i .\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   A point \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_i\\) iff \\(b=a+i\\).  \n   The condition \\(a+b\\le k+1=2t\\) becomes \\(2a+i\\le2t\\), i.e.  \n\n   \\[\n   1\\le a\\le t-\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{i}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   |L_i\\cap T_k|=t-\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{i}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n   \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   *Lines of slope \\(-2\\).*  \n   For \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,t-1\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   M_j:\\;y=-2x+(2t+j) .\n   \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n\n   A point \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(M_j\\) iff \\(b=-2a+2t+j\\).  \n   The inequalities \\(a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1\\) give  \n\n   \\[\n   1\\le a\\le t-\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{j}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n   \\tag{13}\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   |M_j\\cap T_k|=t-\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{j}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n   \\tag{14}\n   \\]\n\n   *Disjointness.*  \n   Suppose a point belongs to both \\(L_i\\) and \\(M_j\\).  \n   Then \\(a+i=-2a+2t+j\\), i.e. \\(3a=2