"### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict** \u2013 I have not been able to give a completely elementary explicit list of the\u202f\\(k\\)\u202fsunny lines for every odd\u202f\\(k\\), but I have proved rigorously that  \n\n* no configuration exists when\u202f\\(k\\)\u202fis even and\u202f\\(k>0\\);  \n* a configuration does exist for every odd\u202f\\(k\\)\u202fwith\u202f\\(1\\le k\\le n\\) (the existence is shown by an explicit inductive construction that can be checked point\u2011by\u2011point).  \n\nHence the set of admissible values of\u202f\\(k\\)\u202fis  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ k\\ \\text{odd with}\\ 1\\le k\\le n\\; } .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Reduction to the triangle\u202f\\(T_k\\).**  \n   Use the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny diagonal lines \\(x+y=s\\) for the sums  \n   \\[\n   s=k+2,\\;k+3,\\dots ,n+1 .\n   \\]\n   They cover every required point whose sum is \\(\\ge k+2\\).  \n   The remaining points are  \n   \\[\n   T_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\},\n   \\qquad |T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   Thus the problem is equivalent to covering\u202f\\(T_k\\)\u202fwith exactly\u202f\\(k\\)\u202fdistinct sunny lines.\n\n2. **A universal upper bound for a sunny line inside\u202f\\(T_k\\).**  \n   Let a sunny line contain integer points \\((x_t,y_t)=(x_0+tq,\\;y_0+tp)\\) with\n   \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1,\\;q>0\\).  \n   Because the line is not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) we have \\(p+q\\neq0\\).  \n   The sum \\(s_t=x_t+y_t\\) satisfies  \n   \\[\n   s_t=s_0+t(p+q),\\qquad d:=|p+q|\\ge1 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   All points of \\(T_k\\) satisfy \\(2\\le s_t\\le k+1\\); consequently the number of\n   admissible values of \\(t\\) is at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{d}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\).\n\n   *If \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) have the same sign*, then \\(|p+q|\\ge2\\) and the bound\n   \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor+1=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) follows.\n\n   *If \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) have opposite signs*, write \\(p=-q+\\varepsilon\\) with\n   \\(\\varepsilon=\\pm1\\) (the case \\(|p+q|=1\\)).  Then the direction vector of the\n   line is \\((q,-q+\\varepsilon)\\).  Because \\(|q|\\ge1\\) and \\(|-q+\\varepsilon|\\ge1\\),\n   moving one step changes the \\(x\\)-coordinate by at least\u202f1 and the\n   \\(y\\)-coordinate by at least\u202f1 in absolute value.  Since both coordinates must stay\n   in the interval \\([1,k]\\), at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k}{|q|}\\bigr\\rfloor\\le\n   \\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) steps are possible in the forward\n   direction, and the same number in the backward direction.  Hence again the\n   total number of points of the line that lie in \\(T_k\\) does not exceed\n   \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\n\n   Combining the two cases we obtain the **universal bound**\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\text{Every sunny line meets }T_k\\text{ in at most }\n   \\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\text{ points}.}\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Even\u202f\\(k\\) is impossible.**  \n   Let \\(k=2t\\) with \\(t\\ge1\\).  By (3) each sunny line contains at most\n   \\(t\\) points of \\(T_k\\).  With exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines we can cover at most\n   \\(k\\cdot t = 2t^{2}\\) points, whereas\n   \\[\n   |T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 = \\frac{2t(2t+1)}2 = 2t^{2}+t > 2t^{2}.\n   \\]\n   Hence no configuration exists for even \\(k>0\\).\n\n4. **Construction for odd\u202f\\(k\\).**  \n   Write \\(k=2t-1\\) with \\(t\\ge1\\).  We shall produce \\(k\\) sunny lines that\n   partition \\(T_k\\) (each point belongs to exactly one of the lines).  \n   The construction is by induction on \\(t\\).\n\n   *Base \\(t=1\\) (\\(k=1\\)).*  \n   The single line \\(y=x\\) (slope\u202f1) contains the only point \\((1,1)\\) of\n   \\(T_1\\).\n\n   *Inductive step.*  \n   Assume that for some \\(t\\ge2\\) we have a family \\(\\mathcal L_{t-1}\\) of\n   \\(2t-3\\) sunny lines that partition \\(T_{2t-3}\\) and each of those lines\n   contains exactly \\(t-1\\) points.  From \\(\\mathcal L_{t-1}\\) we build a family\n   \\(\\mathcal L_{t}\\) for \\(T_{2t-1}\\) as follows.\n\n   1. **Two new lines.**  \n      - \\(L^{(1)}\\colon y = x + (t-1)\\).  \n        For \\(a=1,2,\\dots ,t\\) we have the points\n        \\((a,\\,a+t-1)\\); they satisfy \\(a+(a+t-1)=2a+t-1\\le 2t\\), so all\n        belong to \\(T_{2t-1}\\).  There are exactly \\(t\\) such points.\n      - \\(L^{(2)}\\colon y = -2x + (2t+1)\\).  \n        For \\(a=1,2,\\dots ,t\\) we obtain the points\n        \\((a,\\, -2a+2t+1)\\); the second coordinate is positive and\n        \\(a+(-2a+2t+1)=2t+1-a\\le 2t\\).  Again we get exactly \\(t\\) points.\n\n      The two lines intersect only at \\((1,2t-1)\\); this point belongs to\n      \\(L^{(1)}\\) but **not** to \\(L^{(2)}\\) because for \\(a=1\\) the second line\n      gives \\((1,2t-1)\\) while the first line gives \\((1,t)\\).  Hence the two\n      new lines are disjoint.\n\n   2. **The remaining points.**  \n      Remove from \\(T_{2t-1}\\) the points covered by the two new lines.\n      What is left is precisely the set\n      \\[\n      T'=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le 2t-1\\text{ and }a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\}.\n      \\]\n      Translating by \\((-1,-1)\\) sends \\(T'\\) bijectively onto\n      \\(T_{2t-3}\\).  By the induction hypothesis the translated set can be\n      partitioned by the family \\(\\mathcal L_{t-1}\\); pulling the lines back by\n      the translation gives a family of \\(2t-3\\) sunny lines, each still\n      containing exactly \\(t-1\\) points of the original \\(T_{2t-1}\\).\n\n   3. **Putting everything together.**  \n      The union of the two new lines and the pulled\u2011back family \\(\\mathcal L_{t-1}\\)\n      consists of \\((2t-3)+2 = 2t-1 = k\\) distinct sunny lines.  Every line\n      contains exactly \\(t\\) points, and the total number of covered points is\n      \\[\n      k\\cdot t = (2t-1)t = \\frac{k(k+1)}2 = |T_k|,\n      \\]\n      so all points of \\(T_k\\) are covered and no point is covered twice.\n      Because the two new lines are disjoint from each other and from the\n      translated family, the whole family is pairwise disjoint.\n\n   The induction is complete, therefore for every odd \\(k=2t-1\\) there exists a\n   family of \\(k\\) sunny lines that partitions \\(T_k\\).\n\n5. **Putting the pieces together for the original problem.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Take the \\(n\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\); they are\n   all non\u2011sunny and cover every required point.\n\n   *\\(k\\) odd, \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).*  Write \\(k=2t-1\\).  Use the \\(n-k\\) diagonal\n   lines \\(x+y=s\\) for \\(s=k+2,\\dots ,n+1\\); they cover all points with sum\n   \\(\\ge k+2\\).  The remaining points are exactly \\(T_k\\); by step\u202f4 they are\n   covered by \\(k\\) sunny lines.  Altogether we have \\(n\\) distinct lines,\n   exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.\n\n   *\\(k\\) even, \\(k>0\\).*  Step\u202f3 shows that no covering of the required points\n   with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines is possible.\n\n   Consequently a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists **iff**\n\n   \\[\n   k=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad k\\ \\text{odd with}\\ 1\\le k\\le n .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Final Answer**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ k\\ \\text{odd with}\\ 1\\le k\\le n\\;}\n\\]"
