uent claim that “at most \\(\\lfloor k/|q|\\rfloor\\) steps are possible in the forward direction” ignores the simultaneous restriction on the \\(y\\)-coordinate. For \\(q=1\\) this bound becomes \\(\\lfloor k\\rfloor\\), which is **larger** than the claimed \\(\\lfloor (k+1)/2\\rfloor\\); the argument does not explain how the backward steps reduce the total to \\(\\lfloor (k+1)/2\\rfloor\\).  \n* Consequently the derivation of the universal bound \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) for opposite‑sign direction vectors is incomplete and, as written, incorrect for many admissible \\((p,q)\\).  \n\n**Classification:** **Justification Gap** – the bound (3) is not rigorously established for all sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Even \\(k\\) is impossible\n\n**Quoted text:**  \n> “Let \\(k=2t\\) with \\(t\\ge1\\). By (3) each sunny line contains at most \\(t\\) points of \\(T_k\\). With exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines we can cover at most \\(k\\cdot t = 2t^{2}\\) points, whereas  \n> \\[\n> |T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 = 2t^{2}+t > 2t^{2}.\n> \\]”\n\n**Verification (assuming bound (3) holds):**  \n* For even \\(k=2t\\), \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor = t\\).  \n* Hence each sunny line can cover at most \\(t\\) points of \\(T_k\\).  \n* With \\(k=2t\\) such lines the maximal number of covered points is \\(2t\\cdot t = 2t^{2}\\).  \n* Since \\(|T_k| = 2t^{2}+t\\) exceeds this maximum, a covering is impossible.  \n\n**Conclusion:** The logical deduction is correct **provided** the universal bound (3) is valid. Because step 2 contains a justification gap, the impossibility proof is not fully rigorous, but the argument itself is sound under the (plausible) bound.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Construction for odd \\(k\\)\n\n#### 4.1 Base case\n\n**Quoted text:**  \n> “Base \\(t=1\\) (\\(k=1\\)). The single line \\(y=x\\) (slope 1) contains the only point \\((1,1)\\) of \\(T_1\\).”\n\n**Verification:**  \n* \\(T_1 = \\{(1,1)\\}\\). The line \\(y=x\\) indeed passes through \\((1