tem  \n  \\[\n  y = x + (t-1),\\qquad y = -2x + (2t+1)\n  \\]  \n  gives \\(3x = t+2\\), i.e. \\(x = \\frac{t+2}{3}\\) and \\(y = \\frac{4t-1}{3}\\).  \n* For integer \\(t\\ge2\\) this intersection point is **not an integer point** (except possibly when \\(t\\equiv1\\pmod 3\\), but even then the coordinates are not of the form \\((1,2t-1)\\)).  \n* The point \\((1,2t-1)\\) lies on \\(L^{(2)}\\) (by plugging \\(a=1\\) into the parametrisation of \\(L^{(2)}\\)) but **does not** satisfy the equation of \\(L^{(1)}\\) unless \\(t=1\\).  \n* Therefore the statement that the two lines intersect only at \\((1,2t-1)\\) and that they are disjoint is **incorrect**.\n\n#### 4.4 Translation of the remaining points and use of the induction hypothesis\n\n**Quoted text:**  \n> “Remove from \\(T_{2t-1}\\) the points covered by the two new lines. What is left is precisely the set  \n> \\[\n> T'=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le 2t-1\\text{ and }a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\}.\n> \\]  \n> Translating by \\((-1,-1)\\) sends \\(T'\\) bijectively onto \\(T_{2t-3}\\). By the induction hypothesis the translated set can be partitioned by the family \\(\\mathcal L_{t-1}\\); pulling the lines back by the translation gives a family of \\(2t-3\\) sunny lines, each still containing exactly \\(t-1\\) points of the original \\(T_{2t-1}\\).”\n\n**Verification (assuming the previous steps were correct):**  \n\n* The description of \\(T'\\) is accurate: after removing points with either \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\) (i.e. the first row and first column) from \\(T_{2t-1}\\) we obtain exactly those points with \\(a,b\\ge2\\) and \\(a+b\\le2t-1\\).  \n* The translation \\((a,b)\\mapsto(a-1,b-1)\\) maps \\(T'\\) onto \\(\\{(a',b')\\mid a',b'>0,\\;a'+b'\\le 2t-3\\}=T_{2t-3}\\).  \n* If a family \\(\\mathcal L_{t-1}\\) of sunny lines partitions \\(T_{2t-3}\\), then translating each line back yields a family of sunny lines partitioning \\(T'\\). The slopes are unchanged, so the lines remain sunny.  \n\n**Issue:** This part of the argument **depends critically** on the correctness of the preceding steps (the exact number of points removed by the two new lines and the claim that the new lines are disjoint from the translated family). Because the count of points removed is wrong (see error in \\(L^{(1)}\\)) and the intersection claim is false, the induction step does **not** produce a partition of \\(T_{2t-1}\\). Hence the construction fails.\n\n#### 4.5 Overall conclusion of the inductive construction\n\n**Quoted text:**  \n> “The union of the two new lines and the pulled‑back family \\(\\mathcal L_{t-1}\\) consists of \\((2t-3)+2 = 2t-1 = k\\) distinct sunny lines. Every line contains exactly \\(t\\) points, and the total number of covered points is  \n> \\[\n> k\\cdot t = (2t-1)t = \\frac{k(k+1)}2 = |T_k|,\n> \\]  \n> so all points of \\(T_k\\) are covered and no point is covered twice.”\n\n**Verification & Critical Errors:**  \n\n* Because \\(L^{(1)}\\) actually contains only \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{t+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points, the total number of points covered by the three families is  \n  \\[\n  (2t-3)(t-1) \\;+\\; t \\;+\\; \\bigl\\lfloor\\tfrac{t+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor \\;<\\; (2t-1)t,\n  \\]  \n  for every \\(t\\ge2\\).  \n* The claim that the two new lines are disjoint from each other and from the pulled‑back family is false (the intersection claim is erroneous). Consequently the “no point is covered twice’’ statement is unsubstantiated.  \n* Hence the inductive construction does **not** achieve a partition of \\(T_{2t-1}\\); the existence of a configuration for odd \\(k\\) remains unproved.\n\n**Classification:** **Critical Error** – the construction collapses, invalidating the existence claim for all odd \\(k\\ge3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 5. Final synthesis of the answer\n\n**Quoted text:**  \n> “Consequently a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists **iff**  \n> \\[\n> k=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad k\\ \\text{odd with}\\ 1\\le k\\le n .\n> \\]”\n\n**Verification